#{\footnote voices}${\footnote Voices and Layers}+{\footnote main:060}K{\footnote voices;layers;harmony;unison;transpose;recorders, number of;pool;Add Layer;Remove Layer}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Voices and Layers\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Layers\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A {\ul layer}{\v term_layer} is one part of the sound. It has a source sound, its steps and its voice genes. Each note plays all layers at the same time. In the DNA, a slash ({\f1 /}) separates layers.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each layer has its own recorders, its {\ul pool}{\v term_pool}. The strips show the color of their layer. To select a layer, click it in the Layers list. Menu commands change the selected layer.\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab {\b Layers > Add Layer} adds a layer with xpding.wav.\par
\pard\sa60\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab {\b Layers > Remove Layer} removes the selected layer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Voices\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The Voices menu changes how the recorders of the selected layer play.\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Transpose\tab Up or down an octave ({\f1 T}). Range -48 to +48 semitones.\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Harmony\tab Each note plays one voice for each interval ({\f1 H}). See {\ul harmony}{\v term_harmony}.\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Unison\tab Each harmony voice becomes 1 to 3 voices, a little out of tune ({\f1 U}). See {\ul unison}{\v term_unison}.\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 More Recorders\tab Gives the layer one more recorder ({\f1 #}).\par
\pard\sa60\li2275\ri175\fi-2160\tx2275\plain\f0\fs20 Fewer Recorders\tab Gives the layer one less recorder.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The harmony choices are: Single note, Note + octave up, Note + octave down, Octaves both ways, Fifth, Major triad, Minor triad and Power stack.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} Harmony and unison use more recorders for each note. With many voices, notes take recorders from each other sooner.\par
\page
#{\footnote effects}${\footnote Effects and Edits}+{\footnote main:070}K{\footnote effects;Effects menu;Edit menu;echo;reverse;speed;volume;Insert File;Mix with File;Copy;Paste Insert;Paste Mix;Delete Before;Delete After;position}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Effects and Edits\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 These are the real commands of Sound Recorder. Each command adds a step to the DNA of the selected layer. Windows XP then runs the steps in Sound Recorder, in order.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Effects menu\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Increase Volume (by 25%)\tab {\f1 +}\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Decrease Volume\tab {\f1 -}\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Increase Speed (by 100%)\tab {\f1 >}  Half the length, an octave up.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Decrease Speed\tab {\f1 <}  Two times the length, an octave down.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Add Echo\tab {\f1 E}\par
\pard\sa60\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Reverse\tab {\f1 R}\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Edit menu\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Copy\tab {\f1 C}  Copies the whole sound of the layer.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Paste Insert\tab {\f1 P}  Inserts the copy at the position.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Paste Mix\tab {\f1 X}  Mixes the copy in at the position.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Insert File\tab {\f1 I}{\f1\i name}  Inserts another sound at the position.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Mix with File\tab {\f1 M}{\f1\i name}  Mixes another sound in at the position.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Move Position To\tab {\f1 @}{\f1\i NN}  Moves the slider to NN% of the length.\par
\pard\sa35\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Delete Before Current Position\tab {\f1 [}\par
\pard\sa60\li3355\ri175\fi-3240\tx3355\plain\f0\fs20 Delete After Current Position\tab {\f1 ]}\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The position\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The position starts at 0 when the source sound loads. Each step leaves it where Sound Recorder leaves it. Only {\f1 @} moves it on purpose. If Paste comes before Copy in a layer, the copy is the source sound as it loaded.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Example: {\f1 tada@20[]} keeps the middle of tada.wav: move to 20%, delete before, then delete after.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa35\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb The Sounds}{\v sounds}\par
