#{\footnote term_dna}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b DNA}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One line of text that is the whole sound: layers of Windows sounds, each changed by Sound Recorder commands in order, and the settings for voices and the envelope.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_recorder}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b recorder}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One of the sixteen Sound Recorders in Windows XP. Each recorder holds one sound and plays one voice at a time.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_layer}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b layer}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One part of the sound, with its own source sound, steps and recorders. Each note plays all layers.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_pool}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b pool}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The recorders that belong to one layer. Set the number with {\f1 #}, or with More Recorders and Fewer Recorders.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_harmony}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b harmony}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Intervals in semitones that each note plays together. {\f1 H0,4,7} plays a major triad. {\f1 H0,12,-12} plays the note and an octave on each side.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_unison}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b unison}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 More voices of the same note, each a few cents out of tune, for a wide sound. {\f1 U3~12} plays 3 voices over 12 cents up and down.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_env}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b envelope}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 How the volume of a note changes: Attack (time to full volume), Decay (time to the sustain level), Sustain (the level while you hold the key) and Release (time to silence after you let go). The Sound Recorder hook sets the volume of each voice in steps of about 10 ms.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_latency}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b latency}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The fixed delay between a note and its sound. The plugin tells the DAW the delay, and the DAW moves recorded parts back to the beat.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_steal}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b voice stealing}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 When all recorders of a layer play, a new note stops the oldest one, with a 5 ms fade, and uses it.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_daw}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b DAW}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A digital audio workstation: the music program that loads the plugin, for example Ableton Live, Bitwig, Cubase, FL Studio, Logic Pro or REAPER.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_root}
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b root note}\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The MIDI note that plays a sound at the rate it was recorded. Set it with {\f1 K} in the globals. The default is 60, middle C.\par
\page
